Run touch flings on Flutter's scroll simulations.

What: adds `gestures/physics.rs`, a Rust translation of Flutter's `Tolerance`, `Simulation`, `FrictionSimulation`
and `ClampingScrollSimulation` (flutter/flutter 3.50.1, ae9b586f34; names, constants and comments kept), exported
from the crate root. `ScrollPhysics` keeps its public API (`Exponential { decay_per_ms }`, `FrictionSpline
{ friction, pixels_per_inch }`, `ios()`, `android()`), but its private `fling_duration`/`fling_distance` give way to
`ScrollPhysics::simulation(speed)`, which builds what Flutter's `createBallisticSimulation` builds for a position in
range: iOS a `FrictionSimulation` with drag `decay_per_ms^1000` (0.998^1000, Flutter's "~0.135"), Android a
`ClampingScrollSimulation` (`pixels_per_inch` stands in for Flutter's 160 logical pixels per inch). `Momentum` holds
that simulation and ends on the first tick it is done at, as Flutter's ballistic scroll does; the tolerance is
Flutter's `toleranceFor` at a device pixel ratio of 2 (velocity 10 px/s, GPUI's `MOMENTUM_STOP_VELOCITY`). The
transcribed AOSP `friction_spline` module and its table test go; the "fling curves are sane" test is rewritten on the
simulations. Ports Flutter's tests as tables, named after the Flutter test per row: all 3 of
`friction_simulation_test.dart`, `test_friction` and `scroll_with_inf_edge_ends` of `newton_test.dart`, all 5 of
`scroll_simulation_test.dart`, and the bounce/clamp rows of `scroll_physics_test.dart`'s "doesn't alter the velocity
for time 0".

Why: Flutter's tests fail on the old maths. Android's fling was AOSP's `SplineOverScroller` curve, which Flutter
replaced because it is not ballistic (flutter/flutter#113424, #120338): its starting velocity is not the release
velocity (2863.6 px/s for a 2866.9 px/s release), it stops with a jump (from 4.9 px/s to 0; a 187 px/s step in one
frame against the test's 85.5 bound), and a fling restarted from its own position and velocity does not retrace it.
Flutter's `ClampingScrollSimulation` travels the same total distance and passes. iOS used the same exponential law as
Flutter's `FrictionSimulation`, which now runs it directly; its `finalX`, `timeAtX` and constant deceleration come
with it, since Flutter's iOS overscroll and macOS preset build on them. Overscroll and bounce at the edges are not
part of this patch. Not platform-specific, so no cfg.

Upstream: not filed (upstreaming paused, AGENTS.md patch rule 14).

